--- Notes-README@f0
+++ Notes-README@installation
@@ -1,4 +1,12 @@
 # Local Notes — working development version
+
+## Current source epoch: browser draft recovery
+
+The current source includes bounded browser recovery copies for unsaved note and to-do drafts. The exact experimental source `f0ca90d2cfafcc10f102c88b7dce3ef3c895d269` passed 128 finite checks and 12 Chromium scenarios on verified hosted Windows. The twelve scenarios comprise four user workflows and eight native IndexedDB cases. The [qualified checkpoint](https://github.com/Ginderlax/rea-research-lab/blob/4107879691f5480b0ac9a5ea29f360ee336504a3/products/rea-studio/results/notes-draft-recovery-20261018/CHECKPOINT.md) preserves those exact receipts and the original failed attempt. Installation requires its own source binding, retained earlier browser assertions and complete-suite evidence; earlier results are never rebound to this source.
+
+Copies remain plaintext in this browser, separated by origin and a stable workspace UUID, with at most four records of 2 MiB each and 8 MiB total. They preserve raw title, body, tags, notebook selection and draft type, without attachment binaries. Recovery requires explicit acknowledgement and opens a separate unsaved note or to-do. Only an explicit Save persists it. An earlier Save may already have committed, so the interface warns users to check saved notes before deliberately creating a separate copy. Recovery never automatically replays an uncertain request or overwrites the original saved note. A missing notebook produces a destination warning.
+
+Unavailable or corrupt browser storage leaves the active editor and ordinary Save available. Clearing browser data or stopping the browser before a write commits can still lose text. These copies are excluded from saved-note exports and do not provide encrypted exported backups, original Joplin autosave/crash lifecycle parity, complete browser compatibility or accessibility acceptance. The earlier proposal sections below retain their historical scope; current acceptance comes only from receipts bound to the corresponding source epoch.
 
 The prospective inline-confirmation candidate replaces native browser dialogs for notebook Trash and Restore with visible Confirm/Cancel controls. It names the selected notebook and recursive scope, initially focuses Cancel, supports Escape cancellation and a two-button Tab cycle, and makes the background inert while pending. After Confirm, both choices are disabled and focus stays on the pending dialog until the request settles. Cancel and closing Notes before Confirm submit no notebook operation. Closing after a committed request suppresses its old view publication; it does not undo the commit. Dirty drafts must still be saved first. Backend notebook recursion, revision and confirmation contracts are unchanged. Other actions retain their existing native dialogs. DOM model tests verify finite keyboard/focus behavior; actual browser and assistive-technology acceptance require separate review.
 
@@ -56,7 +64,7 @@
 
 Still missing: full CommonMark/Joplin Markdown compatibility, rich-text editing, inline image rendering, heading anchors and external Joplin URL handlers, complete attachment preview/download policies and automatic resource cleanup, encryption/key management, synchronization and conflicts, native/mobile/terminal/web clients, web clipper, plugin APIs, to-do alarms and editing controls, geolocation UI, complete notebook lifecycle and original drag/drop behavior, sharing/publishing, complete JEX/RAW and other format interoperability, OCR, advanced search/ranking, autosave/crash recovery, accessibility parity, profiles and original client/service APIs. Strict archive rejection and bounded fixture checks are not evidence of full import/export parity or superiority.
 
-Notes and binaries remain plaintext locally. Unsaved drafts are lost on navigation/reload. JSON import creates fresh note IDs/timestamps and rejects Joplin metadata or internal links because that format cannot preserve related binaries/identities. JSON and standalone Markdown exports exclude resource binaries; the UI identifies JSON as incomplete backup material. A crash between binary preparation and metadata commit may leave an unreferenced binary file; it does not expose a partially imported note state. No automatic permanent purge is provided. All undocumented or untested behavior remains an open acceptance gap.
+Notes and binaries remain plaintext locally. Unsaved drafts can have bounded plaintext browser recovery copies; restoring one requires explicit separate-copy acknowledgement and another Save. A browser stop before the write commits or clearing browser data can still lose the draft. JSON import creates fresh note IDs/timestamps and rejects Joplin metadata or internal links because that format cannot preserve related binaries/identities. JSON and standalone Markdown exports exclude resource binaries; the UI identifies JSON as incomplete backup material. A crash between binary preparation and metadata commit may leave an unreferenced binary file; it does not expose a partially imported note state. No automatic permanent purge is provided. All undocumented or untested behavior remains an open acceptance gap.
 
 The retained [original scope](results/notebook-trash-followup/stage/README.md.snapshot), [source contracts](results/notebook-trash-followup/stage/SOURCE-CONTRACTS.md.snapshot) and [notice](results/notebook-trash-followup/stage/NOTICE.md.snapshot) bind the additional Desktop 3.6.14 study. Original code remains test-only; the finite runtime is source-assisted and has no full notebook parity claim. The new exact helper comparisons use memory adapters and a fixed clock, not the original application/database/GUI.
 
